 *   **Kernel Degeneracy:** If a kernel center moves outside the Gaussian support, the method degenerates to standard 2DGS behavior for that primitive. The sources state that no special restrictions are imposed to prevent this, as it rarely occurs (probability of remaining inside is >99.5% across datasets) [S1].
 *   **Weight Zero Division:** If all exponential weights are effectively zero (e.g., intersection point is very far from all kernels), normalization requires a fallback (e.g., uniform distribution) to avoid division by zero [G].
-*   **Gradient Vanishing:** While not explicitly detailed as a pitfall for movable kernels (unlike bilinear interpolation), the exponential weighting can lead to vanishing gradients for kernels far from the intersection point if $\lambda_e$ is too large [S1]. The default $\lambda_e = 0.1$ is chosen to balance this [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### SVGS with Bilinear Interpolation
-Uses four fixed kernels located in the four quadrants of the elliptical Gaussian surfel, assigning learnable color and opacity to each quadrant via bilinear interpolation. Simpler but may cause gradient vanishing. Choose when computational simplicity is prioritized over maximum expressiveness. [Link](8729-spatially-varying-gaussian-splatting-svgs.md#bilinear-interpolation)
